course: religion-ethics
lesson: 71
title: Scary films, games, hospitals and what helps
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many fears about death come from things we watch, play or hear rather than from real life: Jack sees a film at his cousin's where a ghost kills people and cannot sleep for days, yet the ghost was made by actors and special effects and cannot hurt anyone.
explain: In games your character «dies» over and over and you just restart, but real death is different: it is permanent, which is exactly why real life is precious.
explain: Some fears grow from one sad story: Mia is scared of the hospital because her grandad died there, but hospitals help and heal far more people than they lose; Ella won't swim after hearing about a child drowning, but swimming lessons and lifeguards make swimming safer.
explain: The internet and the news can make rare dangers feel close: a «killer clown» story is made up to scare people, and a terrible earthquake on the news happened far away, while big earthquakes are very rare in Britain.
explain: What helps: telling a grown-up, choosing films for your age, a night-light, slow breathing, a hug, and for Christians a prayer or a verse like «Do not be afraid, for I am with you».
explain: Being scared of dying is not babyish: many grown-ups feel it, and Christians remember that even Jesus was afraid in the garden of Gethsemane the night before he died.
fact: Films are made by actors and special effects; film ghosts are not real and cannot hurt anyone.
fact: Age ratings on films and games help families choose what is right for a child's age.
fact: In games, death is pretend and you can restart; real death is permanent.
fact: Most people who go to hospital get better; hospitals are mainly places of help and healing.
fact: Many hospitals have chaplains from different faiths who comfort patients and families.
fact: Many scary internet stories, such as «killer clown» rumours, are made up to frighten people.
fact: Big earthquakes happen mostly in certain parts of the world and are very rare in Britain.
fact: Swimming lessons and lifeguards keep people safer in the water.
fact: Houses creak as they cool down at night or when the wind blows; creaks are not ghosts.
fact: Halloween's name comes from All Hallows' Eve, the night before All Saints' Day; it may also have roots in an old Celtic festival called Samhain.
fact: Some Christians enjoy Halloween, while some churches hold «light parties» instead.
fact: Many grown-ups are scared of dying too; Christians say Jesus himself was afraid in the garden of Gethsemane.
exercise: Jack can't sleep after a film where a ghost kills people. Is the ghost real? | No; it was made by actors and special effects | Yes; films only ever show things that really happened | Yes, but it only appears at night when you're alone | No, but it can still come into his room after dark
exercise: What can help Jack after the scary film? | Telling a grown-up and choosing films for his age | Watching the same film again, alone in his room, to stop being scared | Keeping the light off to be brave | Promising never to sleep again
exercise: In Noah's game his character dies over and over. Is that like real dying? | No; in games you restart, but real death is permanent | Yes; real people also restart after dying | Yes, it is exactly the same feeling | No, because real people never die at all
exercise: Mia is scared to visit her gran in hospital because Grandad died there. What is true? | Most people in hospital get better | Most people who go to hospital die there | Hospitals make people more ill | Children catch death in hospitals
exercise: Who in many hospitals comforts patients and families of different faiths? | Chaplains | Lifeguards | Referees | Shopkeepers
exercise: Charlie's older brother tells him about a «killer clown» on the internet. What is true? | Scary internet stories are often made up to frighten | Killer clowns live in every town and come out at night | Everything posted online is true | Clowns are always dangerous people
exercise: What should Charlie do about scary things he sees online? | Tell a grown-up | Keep it secret from everyone | Share it with younger kids | Search for more of them
exercise: Zoe saw a terrible earthquake on the news. Is one likely in her British town? | Very unlikely; big earthquakes are very rare in Britain | Yes, earthquakes happen everywhere equally | Yes, next week, because it was on the news | No, because Britain has no ground under it
exercise: After hearing about a child drowning, Ella won't swim. What makes swimming safer? | Swimming lessons and lifeguards | Never going near any water again, ever | Swimming only at night, when it's quiet | Swimming alone in deep water to get brave
exercise: Leo hears creaking at night and is sure it's a ghost. What is it most likely? | The house creaking as it cools or in the wind | A ghost walking slowly up and down on the stairs | A dead relative coming back at night to visit him | A monster living under the floorboards of his room
exercise: What can help Leo feel safer at night? | A night-light, and talking about it in the morning | Hiding under the bed until the morning comes and it gets light | Shouting at the ghost to go away | Reading ghost stories until he falls asleep
exercise: Sophie asks what helps when she's scared of dying. Which helps? | Talking, hugging, breathing slowly, thinking of happy things | Keeping it all inside and never talking to anyone about it at all | Watching scary films to get used to it | Searching the internet about death alone
exercise: Which Bible words do Christians often say when they're scared? | «Do not be afraid, for I am with you» | «Run away and never look back» | «Fear is a sin that cannot be forgiven» | «Only the brave will go to heaven»
exercise: Where does the name «Halloween» come from? | All Hallows' Eve, the night before All Saints' Day | A famous witch called Hallo Ween | The first person ever to dress as a ghost | A Christmas song sung on 31 October
exercise: Max gets a fright from a skeleton costume at Halloween. What is it? | Just a costume for dressing up | A real skeleton from a graveyard | A sign that someone will die | A ghost wearing a disguise
exercise: Kai asks if it's babyish to be scared of dying. What is true? | No; many grown-ups are scared of dying too | Yes; only babies are scared of things | Yes; brave people never feel fear | No, because nobody is ever scared of dying
test: «That ghost film was bussin' but now I can't sleep, is the ghost real??» asks Jack's cousin in Atlanta (bussin' = really good). Best answer? | No; it's actors and effects, and it can't hurt you | Yes, film ghosts are based on real ones that people have seen | Only if you watch it more than once | Yes, but they only come out at midnight
test: «I died like 40 times in Fortnite lol, dying's easy,» says Noah's friend. What's true? | Game deaths restart; real death is permanent | Real dying is just like the game, you just come back | He's right, dying is really no big deal at all | Game deaths are a warning that real ones are coming
test: «Hospitals are proper grim, people peg it there,» says a boy in Birmingham (peg it = die). What's the truth? | Most people in hospital get better and go home | Nobody ever comes out of hospital alive, not even the doctors | Hospitals are only for people who are dying | Visiting a hospital makes children ill
test: A Gen Alpha boy says, «the killer clown is coming to our school, it's on TikTok, no cap.» Best response? | Scary online stories are often made up; tell a grown-up | Stay home from school for a week until the police have caught it | Share it so everyone is ready | Believe it because lots of people saw it
test: What would happen if Ella never learned to swim because she was scared? | She'd stay less safe near water; lessons make her safer | She would be completely safe from all danger | She would become a better swimmer | Nothing; swimming skills don't matter, because you can always just stay on the beach
test: «Wee man, that's no' a ghost, it's just the hoose creakin',» says a Glasgow grandad (wee man = little lad, hoose = house). Is he right? | Yes; houses creak as they cool and in wind | No; creaking is always a ghost moving about the house | No; creaks mean that someone in the house has just died | Yes, but only in very old houses with wooden floors
test: The vicar announces, very formally: «Our Halloween light party shall be held in the church hall, all being welcome.» What is it? | A church party as an alternative to spooky Halloween | A service to scare away ghosts from the village | A meeting to ban costumes for ever | A funeral held on 31 October
test: In a cartoon, the hero survives being squashed flat and pops back up. What's true about real life? | Real bodies can't just pop back; real life is precious | Real people can pop back up like cartoons if they are strong | Cartoon rules work for children only | Being squashed is never dangerous
test: «Is it babyish to be scared of dying? Like, cringe?» asks Kai (cringe = embarrassing). What's the best answer? | No; lots of grown-ups feel it, even Jesus felt afraid | Yes; only little kids feel that fear | Yes; real men are never afraid of anything, not even dying | No, because nobody ever thinks about dying
test: «Yous are watchin' the news again, now the wee one's scared of earthquakes,» says Zoe's Belfast nan (yous = you all). What should the family tell Zoe? | Big earthquakes are very rare here; she is safe | Earthquakes will hit their town very soon | The news is always about her own town | She should sleep out in the garden in a tent to be safe
test: Myth to check: «If a film shows it, it could happen to you tonight.» | False; films are made-up stories | True; films show real dangers nearby | True, but only horror films | False, but only for cartoons
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the depiction of death in computer games? | Death in games is pretended and reversible, whereas real death is permanent | Death in games is equivalent in every respect to real death | Death in games causes real harm to the player | Death in games is prohibited by law in the United Kingdom
exam: Identify the principal function of a hospital chaplain. | To provide comfort and spiritual care to patients and their families | To perform surgical operations upon patients | To determine which patients shall be admitted | To supervise the hospital's financial accounts
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the creaking of houses at night. | It results from the cooling of materials and the action of wind | It results from the presence of the spirits of the dead | It indicates that an earthquake is imminent | It occurs only in houses where a death has taken place
exam: Whereas a child reports fear of drowning, ascertain the measure most consistent with the matters taught for increasing safety. | Instruction in swimming under the supervision of lifeguards | Permanent avoidance of all bodies of water, including the sea, rivers and pools | Swimming unaccompanied in deep water | Swimming exclusively during the hours of darkness
